\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\title{States and pullback of observables}
\date{2026-10-23}

\p{A law on a finite set of configurations assigns weights to possible
fields and averages their observables. The
[sixteen binary fields on the four-cycle](fdip-0029) give a concrete
example: summing weights over configurations with the same retained
values produces the retained law. Observable algebras and normalized
functionals express this operation without assuming a finite
configuration set.}

\p{A supplied algebra map pulls a functional back by precomposition.
Over the reals or complexes, positivity and the involution determine
which functionals are states; the [real dual-number example](fdip-0025)
explains why the hypotheses matter. Coordinate restriction then pulls
polynomial observables back. For real fields with infinitely many
configurations, [reference measure](fdip-003U) and
[Gaussian integrability](fdip-0017) give the corresponding expectations.}

